# Derived artifacts: a merge of two branches is never the answer for them.
#
# Every path below is written by `scripts/derive` out of canonical sources that git merges
# perfectly well. What conflicts is the *fingerprint* the generators stamp into the output —
# `Generated from canonical inputs <hash>`, `"source_hash": ...` — and that value is a
# function of the merged tree, so neither side's is right and no textual combination of them
# is either. Left to the default driver, every branch that touches a canonical file conflicts
# here, and the resolution is always the same gesture: take a side, run `scripts/derive`,
# stage the real answer. One branch open today has done it five times.
#
# `merge=derived` short-circuits the pretence. The driver (scripts/merge-derived) resolves to
# ours and exits clean, so the merge completes and the tree carries a *stale* derived file —
# which is exactly what the `derived-current` gate in .githooks/pre-commit refuses to let you
# commit (`scripts/pages current`, and `scripts/derive-check` behind it in CI). The staleness
# is caught by the thing built to catch staleness, one command later, instead of by hand
# inside a conflict marker.
#
# The driver has to be declared per clone — `git config merge.derived.driver ...`, which
# `just derive-merge-driver` does. Without that declaration git falls back to the default
# text merge and you get the conflicts you get today: this file makes merging better where it
# is wired and never worse where it is not.

docs/PLAN_STATUS.md                 merge=derived
docs/PAGES_STATUS.md                merge=derived
docs/SITE_CLAIMS.md                 merge=derived
docs/generated/**                   merge=derived
site/data/generated/**              merge=derived
site/data/registry/**               merge=derived
# The public release metadata: rendered from the record under .ai/repo/releases/ by
# `majordomus generate`, one file per release plus the stable pointer. They arrived with the
# first real release and had no rule until then, so every merge touching a release conflicted
# on them — and the pointer changes on every release, which is exactly when several branches
# are in flight. test/cases/57_derived_merge_driver.sh is what noticed.
site/static/releases/**             merge=derived
# Every page under site/content/docs/ is written by scripts/generate-site-data out of the
# CLI tree, the capability registry and the documents: the front matter carries a `weight`
# derived from presentation order, so any branch that adds a command renumbers pages it
# never touched. Merging two renumberings is never the answer; deriving is.
site/content/docs/**                merge=derived
site/content/docs/pages-status.md   merge=derived
site/content/docs/plan-status.md    merge=derived
site/content/docs/site-claims.md    merge=derived

# The site's registry projection: one page per capability and per module, all of them
# rewritten whenever the registry moves.
site/content/registry/**            merge=derived

# The whole documentation tree the site serves: `scripts/generate-site-data` deletes and
# rewrites site/content/docs on every run — the long-form pages are the canonical Markdown
# under docs/ with a generated front matter, and the CLI reference is one page per command
# whose `weight` is a function of where that command sits in the whole list. Adding a
# command anywhere renumbers every page after it, so two branches that each add one
# conflict on every page between them, over a number neither of them decides.
site/content/docs/**                merge=derived

# The schema, allow-list and section projections: `majordomus generate` writes all three out
# of the kind and capability declarations, and they conflict for the same reason the rest of
# this file exists — the merged tree's answer appears in neither side.
# Only the projected schemas: the `.proto` beside each one is a hand-written source, and
# merging two edits of a source is exactly what git should do with it.
# Only the schemas `majordomus generate` actually writes, named one by one. The glob that
# used to stand here — share/schemas/**/*.schema.json — covered all of them, and generate
# writes nine of forty-five: the other thirty-six are hand-authored, and the driver resolved
# each merge by taking one whole generation and discarding the other side in silence. It ate
# the policy schema twice in one evening; the second time the merged tree refused to serve.
# The authority is docs/generated/artifacts.json. When generate gains or loses a schema this
# list must follow it:
#   grep -o '"share/schemas/[^"]*schema.json"' docs/generated/artifacts.json | tr -d '"' | sort -u
share/schemas/majordomus/adr/adr.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/application/application.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/capture/capture.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/context/context.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/knowledge/knowledge.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/prompt/prompt.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/rule/rule.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/skill/skill.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/schemas/majordomus/use-case/use-case.v1.schema.json      merge=derived
share/allow/**                      merge=derived
share/sections/**                   merge=derived

# The provider projections. They carry a stamp over the policy hash and their own content,
# so a merge of two branches leaves a stamp that describes neither.
AGENTS.md                           merge=derived
CLAUDE.md                           merge=derived
.bb/AGENTS.md                       merge=derived

# The installer and the document that describes it: both are written out of the release and
# platform declarations, and both carry a fingerprint of the tree they were generated from.
docs/INSTALL.md                     merge=derived
site/static/install.sh              merge=derived

# The release metadata the site publishes: `majordomus generate distribution` writes one
# file per release plus latest.json out of the release declarations, so two branches that
# cut or record a release conflict here on content neither of them decides.
site/static/releases/**             merge=derived

# The deployment artifacts: `majordomus generate` writes fly.toml, deploy/Dockerfile and
# .dockerignore out of .ai/repo/deployments/majordomus.yaml. They landed with the deployment
# module and had no rule, so a merge of two branches that each moved the deployment model
# conflicted on three files whose content neither side decides.
fly.toml                            merge=derived
deploy/Dockerfile                   merge=derived
.dockerignore                       merge=derived

# The design system's projections: `majordomus generate` writes the token declaration, the
# stylesheets and the marks out of share/design/tokens.yaml, and each carries the design
# fingerprint of the tree it was generated from. They landed with the design-system model
# and with no rule of their own, so a merge of two branches that each touched a token
# conflicted on ten files whose content neither side decides — the same failure the
# deployment artifacts had, in a new subsystem. The inventory in
# docs/generated/artifacts.json is what noticed; test/cases/57_derived_merge_driver.sh asks
# git about every path in it.
apps/majordomus-cli/src/design/tokens.yaml   merge=derived
apps/majordomus-cli/src/web/tokens.css       merge=derived
apps/majordomus-cli/src/cockpit/logo-mark.svg merge=derived
share/cockpit/favicon.svg           merge=derived
share/design/*.css                  merge=derived
site/static/favicon.svg             merge=derived
site/static/images/logo.svg         merge=derived
site/static/images/logo-mark.svg    merge=derived
